<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Poland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Polish</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Poland is a country in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. United with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, it formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> from 1569, one of the largest states in Europe, until its partition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in the late eighteenth century. It was restored as an independent state in 1918 from territories of the Russian, German and Austro-Hungarian empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/poland.jpg" alt="Poland" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See below. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Poland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the fall, 30,000 wealthy Armenians moved to Lusignan Cyprus and merchant families founded or joined diaspora communities in France, Italy, the Netherlands, <b>Poland</b> and Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…I. The title gave a common name to the composite monarchy the Habsburgs had built since the sixteenth century: the Austrian hereditary lands, the Lands of the Bohemian Crown, the Kingdom of Hungary with Croatia, the Grand Principality of Transylvania, and Galicia, taken in the partitions of <b>Poland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire's break-up in 1918 created or enlarged seven states, Austria, Hungary, Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia, <b>Poland</b>, Romania and Italy, along lines that satisfied the principle of national self-determination only imperfectly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>With these the original Mongol armies had conquered peoples from China to <b>Poland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Visitors found the Hetmanate unusually literate: in the 1740s 866 of 1,099 settlements in seven regimental districts had primary schools, more per head than in Russia or <b>Poland</b>, and Paul of Aleppo wrote in 1654 that even wives and daughters could read.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Frontiers were fixed only after fighting: troops occupied the German-speaking borderlands of Bohemia and Moravia, defended Slovakia against the Hungarian Soviet Republic in 1919 and clashed with <b>Poland</b> over Teschen, which was divided in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
